Goal: we have to implement the reallocate/transfer the amounts between pots, without effecting the actual net asset units or amounts.

Approach (v2 — DIRECT POSITION ADJUSTMENT, no new ledger transactions):
Backend execution differs from v1 as described below. The UI flow is REVISED from v1 (see
"UI Flow v3 — Smart & Guided" section further down) to minimise the user's mental math and
make errors structurally impossible rather than merely validated-against.
The ONLY backend difference from v1 is what happens on "Proceed":

    v1 (ledger approach): creates new Sell+Buy rows in investments_transactions (source='REALLOCATION'),
        visible in Trades listing with a badge/note, same as Reconciliation trades.

    v2 (this file): NO new rows in investments_transactions at all. Instead, directly adjust
        investments_pot_positions.units for each (pot, account_asset) pair affected — a plain
        "-X units here, +X units there" delta, using the exact same adjustPotPosition() upsert
        already used by investmentsAllocationService.ts. investments_positions (the account-level
        total per asset) is never touched either way, in both v1 and v2 — it was never meant to
        change, since reallocation only moves POT ATTRIBUTION, not real holdings.

    Nothing appears in the Trades listing (Un-labeled or Labeled) for a v2 reallocation — it is not
    a trade. For traceability/audit purposes (so the user can see "when" and "what" moved between
    pots later), a lightweight separate history record was proposed below —

    DECIDED: DEFER this to a later fast-follow. Ship the core move-units functionality first
    (direct investments_pot_positions adjustment, no history table at all), get it tested in real
    use, THEN decide whether a dedicated history table is actually needed. Not part of v1 of this
    feature.

        (Deferred) New table (name TBD, e.g. investments_pot_reallocations):
            id, user_id, account_asset_id, from_pot_id, to_pot_id,
            units, price (price used at the time, just for displaying the rs-equivalent),
            amount (units * price, for display only),
            reallocation_date, created_at

        One row per (asset, source pot, destination pot) movement. Would be shown only in a
        dedicated "Reallocation history" view (e.g. on the Pot detail screen) — never mixed into
        the Trades/Un-labeled/Labeled lists, since this is explicitly NOT a trade.

---------------------------------

Other resolved decisions (eligibility, edge cases, precision):

    - Entry point visibility: the "Modify Allocation" button is HIDDEN entirely (not shown,
      not disabled-with-tooltip — just absent) when the user has only a single pot total, since
      there is nowhere to send/take from. No explanation needed; it simply doesn't render.

    - Only ACTIVE pots are ever eligible anywhere in this flow — as the pot being modified, and as
      every source/target pot offered in Screen 2. Archived pots are never shown as an option at
      all (not merely excluded from sums), matching the existing real-allocation rule in
      investmentsAllocationService.ts.

    - Multi-account assets ARE in scope (not deferred). investments_pot_positions/transactions key
      off (account, asset), not bare asset name — if a pot holds the same fund via 2+ different
      investment accounts, EACH (account, asset) combination is its own line item in Screen 3's
      breakdown, never merged. Broker name is never shown in this journey (deliberately kept out
      of the mental model) — only a short account prefix disambiguates, e.g.:
          "Acc 1 - UTI Nifty 50 Fund"   ₹5.00
          "Acc 2 - UTI Nifty 50 Fund"   ₹5.00
      (same account naming the app already uses elsewhere, e.g. "My Demat Account 1" shortened to
      an "Acc N" style prefix — exact label format TBD at implementation time, just no broker name).

    - Assets with no cached latest price (investments_asset_latest_prices has no row, or it's
      otherwise unusable) are BLOCKED from reallocation entirely — not offered as a line item in
      Screen 3, since there's no reliable rs <-> units conversion without a price. Mirrors how such
      assets already contribute ₹0 to currentValue elsewhere in the app.

    - Rounding/precision rule: units are always rounded to 6 decimals (matching the
      NUMERIC(20,6) column). When converting a rupee split into units (amount ÷ price), the
      ACTUALLY APPLIED amount for display/record purposes is recomputed as rounded_units × price
      (not the originally-typed rupee figure) — the auto-balanced "last field" trick (above)
      already absorbs most of this drift since it's always derived as a remainder, never typed.

---------------------------------

UI Flow v3 — Smart & Guided (replaces v1's 3-screen manual-entry flow)

Problems with the v1 flow this fixes:
    - Screen "distribute across pots" started blank — user had to mentally compute a valid split
      from scratch with no pre-fill.
    - TWO separate "must sum exactly to X" screens (pot-level, then per-pot asset-level) — each is
      an independent place a typo can block progress.
    - No upfront bound on the target amount — user could type an unreachable value and only find
      out several screens later.
    - No per-field max/cap shown — nothing stops typing a deduction bigger than what a pot/asset
      actually holds until a late validation error.
    - Review screen wasn't specified in UI terms — risk of defaulting to a raw wall of Sell/Buy-
      style lines instead of what a user actually thinks in (pot balances, before -> after).

Core trick used everywhere a number must be split across multiple fields: pre-fill every field
with a sensible default, and make the LAST field always read-only / auto-computed as "whatever's
left". This makes an incorrect sum structurally impossible, rather than something to validate
against — there is never a "must match exactly" error, because the last number is never typed
by the user.

Confirmed defaults (decided, not just "recommended"):
    - Pot-level split (Screen 2) default: EQUAL split across all other active pots.
    - Asset-level split (Screen 3) default: EQUAL split across that pot's assets.
    - The auto-computed "last field" is FULLY LOCKED/read-only, always — never tappable/editable.
      If the user wants a different split, they edit one of the OTHER (non-last) fields instead;
      the locked field just keeps re-deriving as "whatever's left". This keeps the "sum is always
      correct" guarantee unconditional, with no escape hatch that could reintroduce a mismatch.

Screen 1 — Target amount:
    - Current amount (big, bold) --> Target amount [input, numeric keypad].
    - Live inline chip updates on every keystroke: "Deduct ₹20" / "Add ₹50" — no separate confirm
      step needed to see the effect of what was just typed.
    - Input is bounded immediately:
        - Decreasing: min = 0.
        - Increasing: max = sum of what all other ACTIVE pots can actually supply right now.
      Helper text under the field states the bound up front, e.g. "Max ₹180 — limited by other
      pots' balances" — never lets the user walk into a dead end deep in the flow.
    - "Next" enabled only once a valid non-zero delta is entered.

Screen 2 — Pot distribution (smart-default, auto-balanced):
    - One row per OTHER active pot: [Pot name] [Current ₹Y] [amount input].
    - Pre-filled automatically the moment this screen opens — default = equal split across all
      other pots. Zero typing required for the common case.
    - All rows except the LAST are freely editable; the LAST row is always greyed-out / read-only,
      showing the auto-computed remainder. Editing any earlier row instantly recalculates it live.
      The sum is therefore always exactly correct by construction.
    - Quick-action chips above the list: "Split equally" (reset to default) / "Proportional to pot
      size" / tap a pot's name to send it 100% in one tap.
    - Each row's input capped at that pot's max feasible contribution (deduct direction only),
      shown inline as "(max ₹Z)" — cannot overshoot.
    - If there is only ONE other eligible pot: SKIP this screen entirely, auto-route 100% to/from
      it, go straight to Screen 3.
    - Live summary line always visible, e.g. "₹20 to distribute — ✓ fully allocated".

Screen 3 — Asset breakdown (shown plainly, not hidden behind a required tap):
    - For each pot involved, show its computed per-asset split directly under its name — default
      is an EQUAL split across that pot's assets, e.g. a pot touching 2 assets for ₹10 total shows:
        "Pot 2 — ₹10:  ₹5.00 Axis Treasury Fund · ₹5.00 UTI Nifty 50 Fund"
      with an "Edit" link to customise — most users never need to tap it.
    - When customising, the SAME auto-balance-last-field trick applies per pot: edit N-1 asset
      amounts, the last auto-fills as the remainder.
    - Deduct-direction inputs capped to that asset's current value within that specific pot, with
      an inline "(max ₹X)" hint and a one-tap "Max" shortcut to fill the ceiling.
    - If a pot only touches a single asset, there is nothing to split — this pot's row shows the
      full amount directly with no edit affordance needed at all.

Screen 4 — Review:
    - Leads with a plain-language Pot-level before -> after table (what a user actually cares
      about), e.g.:
          Pot 1   ₹100 -> ₹80   (−₹20)
          Pot 2   ₹0   -> ₹10   (+₹10)
          Pot 3   ₹0   -> ₹10   (+₹10)
    - Below that, a collapsed "View asset-level detail" section with the granular units/price
      breakdown, for power users / audit curiosity — collapsed by default, never required reading.
    - Single "Proceed" button commits everything atomically.

Net effect: in the common case (one target pot being edited, one or two other pots involved,
each pot holding one or two assets), the flow collapses to effectively 2 real interactive screens
(target amount -> review), with full manual override always available but never required.

---------------------------------

Example scenario (User wants to reduce the amount) — same setup as v1:
Current values:
    - Pot 1: 100rs
        --> asset 1: 6 units, 10rs price = 60rs
        --> asset 2: 8 units, 5rs price = 40rs
    - Pot 2: 0rs
    - Pot 3: 0rs
    Total Asset wise:
        --> asset 1: 6 units, 10rs price = 60rs
        --> asset 2: 8 units, 5rs price = 40rs

User wants to keep only 80rs in Pot 1 (same guided screens as v1, same user inputs):
    Screen 1: Current amount: 100rs --> Target amount: 80rs (Deduct 20rs from Pot 1)
        Transfer to other pots: pot 2: 10rs, pot 3: 10rs
    Screen 2 (Asset-pot breakdown):
        asset1: deduct 15rs -> pot 2: 7.5rs (0.75 units), pot 3: 7.5rs (0.75 units)
        asset2: deduct 5rs  -> pot 2: 2.5rs (0.5 units),  pot 3: 2.5rs (0.5 units)
    Screen 3: Review -> Proceed

Execute (v2 — direct position deltas, no transactions created):
    investments_pot_positions updates:
        Pot 1, asset 1: units -1.5   (6.0  -> 4.5)   [0.75 to pot2 + 0.75 to pot3]
        Pot 1, asset 2: units -1.0   (8.0  -> 7.0)   [0.5 to pot2 + 0.5 to pot3]
        Pot 2, asset 1: units +0.75  (0.0  -> 0.75)
        Pot 3, asset 1: units +0.75  (0.0  -> 0.75)
        Pot 2, asset 2: units +0.5   (0.0  -> 0.5)
        Pot 3, asset 2: units +0.5   (0.0  -> 0.5)

    Check: Pot 1 lost 1.5u asset1 (15rs) + 1.0u asset2 (5rs) = 20rs total -> matches target deduction.
    Check: Pot 2 gained 0.75u asset1 (7.5rs) + 0.5u asset2 (2.5rs) = 10rs -> matches its share.
    Check: Pot 3 gained 0.75u asset1 (7.5rs) + 0.5u asset2 (2.5rs) = 10rs -> matches its share.
    Check: Total units of asset1 across all pots = 4.5+0.75+0.75 = 6.0 (unchanged). Total units of
        asset2 = 7.0+0.5+0.5 = 8.0 (unchanged). investments_positions never touched. ✓

    Optional history rows (investments_pot_reallocations):
        asset1, from pot1, to pot2, 0.75 units, 10rs price, 7.5rs
        asset1, from pot1, to pot3, 0.75 units, 10rs price, 7.5rs
        asset2, from pot1, to pot2, 0.5 units, 5rs price, 2.5rs
        asset2, from pot1, to pot3, 0.5 units, 5rs price, 2.5rs

---------------------------------

Example scenario (User wants to increase the amount) — same setup as v1:
Current values:
    - Pot 1: 100rs
        --> asset 1: 6 units, 10rs price = 60rs
        --> asset 2: 8 units, 5rs price = 40rs
    - Pot 2: 200rs
        --> asset 1: 5 units, 10rs price = 50rs
        --> asset 2: 30 units, 5rs price = 150rs
    - Pot 3: 300rs
        --> asset 1: 5 units, 10rs price = 50rs
        --> asset 2: 50 units, 5rs price = 250rs
    Total Asset wise:
        --> asset 1: 16 units, 10rs price = 160rs
        --> asset 2: 88 units, 5rs price = 440rs

User wants to increase Pot 2 to 250rs (same guided screens as v1, same user inputs):
    Screen 1: Current amount: 200rs --> Target amount: 250rs (Add 50rs to Pot 2)
        Deduct from pots: pot 1: 25rs, pot 3: 25rs
    Screen 2 (Pot-asset breakdown):
        pot 1: deduct 25rs -> asset1: 12.5rs (1.25 units), asset2: 12.5rs (2.5 units)
        pot 3: deduct 25rs -> asset1: 12.5rs (1.25 units), asset2: 12.5rs (2.5 units)
    Screen 3: Review -> Proceed

Execute (v2 — direct position deltas, no transactions created):
    investments_pot_positions updates:
        Pot 1, asset 1: units -1.25  (6.0  -> 4.75)
        Pot 1, asset 2: units -2.5   (8.0  -> 5.5)
        Pot 3, asset 1: units -1.25  (5.0  -> 3.75)
        Pot 3, asset 2: units -2.5   (50.0 -> 47.5)
        Pot 2, asset 1: units +2.5   (5.0  -> 7.5)    [1.25 from pot1 + 1.25 from pot3]
        Pot 2, asset 2: units +5.0   (30.0 -> 35.0)   [2.5 from pot1 + 2.5 from pot3]

    Check: Pot 1 lost 1.25u asset1 (12.5rs) + 2.5u asset2 (12.5rs) = 25rs -> matches its share.
    Check: Pot 3 lost 1.25u asset1 (12.5rs) + 2.5u asset2 (12.5rs) = 25rs -> matches its share.
    Check: Pot 2 gained 2.5u asset1 (25rs) + 5.0u asset2 (25rs) = 50rs -> matches target increase.
    Check: Total units of asset1 across all pots = 4.75+7.5+3.75 = 16.0 (unchanged). Total units of
        asset2 = 5.5+35.0+47.5 = 88.0 (unchanged). investments_positions never touched. ✓

    Optional history rows (investments_pot_reallocations):
        asset1, from pot1, to pot2, 1.25 units, 10rs price, 12.5rs
        asset1, from pot3, to pot2, 1.25 units, 10rs price, 12.5rs
        asset2, from pot1, to pot2, 2.5 units, 5rs price, 12.5rs
        asset2, from pot3, to pot2, 2.5 units, 5rs price, 12.5rs




